PRIVATE REFLECTION: All six colleagues in round 1 continue to converge on answer E, matching the unanimous round 0 consensus. The shared analysis remains that Evergreen Grass Care Ltd is vicariously liable for torts of its servants committed in the course of employment, each employee remains directly liable for his own negligence, giving joint and several liability as between employer and the employee who committed the wrong, with liability assessed separately for each distinct wrong and loss.

Mark – pool/cover/clean-up loss: Mark was cutting grass at Bruce’s property on Evergreen’s instructions operating Evergreen’s riding mower. The fall after reaching down from a moving mower to retrieve a stick thrown to the neighbour’s dog, dislocating his shoulder, and the automatic-drive mower then running uncontrolled onto and through the pool cover is negligent operation of work equipment in the time, place and manner of employment. A momentary personal distraction/play with the dog is a deviation in manner of performance, not a frolic wholly detached from employment; the work, equipment, site and time remain work-related. Mark is directly liable and Evergreen is vicariously liable; Evergreen and Mark are jointly and severally liable for the pool cover replacement, removal and clean-up costs. Bruce’s neighbours’ short fence and the dog are background circumstances only; no duty/causation making neighbours concurrent tortfeasors for the pool loss.

Lincoln – Ferrari scratches loss: Lincoln was raking/blowing leaves with employer equipment at the same site. He “went over the area with the blower as he normally would” on an area of small rocks adjacent to Bruce’s Ferrari, propelling rocks into the car causing barely noticeable scratches. The physical act is the ordinary performance of the assigned leaf-clearing task with employer tools at the work site in work time. Personal animus toward Bruce, admiring the car and noting Bruce’s absence goes to motive but does not convert the negligent use of the blower into a frolic wholly detached from employment. The risk of propelling loose material with a blower is foreseeable in the work. Minimal/barely noticeable damage affects quantum only; de minimis does not bar recovery in Ontario and nominal damages are available. Lincoln is directly liable and Evergreen is vicariously liable; Evergreen and Lincoln are jointly and severally liable for the Ferrari scratch damage.

Separate wrongs give separate joint and several pairings: Evergreen + Mark for Mark’s loss; Evergreen + Lincoln for Lincoln’s loss. Lincoln is not liable for Mark’s loss and Mark is not liable for Lincoln’s loss.

Eliminated alternatives remain consistent: 
A incorrectly makes neighbours jointly and severally liable for Mark’s loss and limits Lincoln’s loss to Lincoln alone; 
B incorrectly makes all three persons jointly and severally liable for all damage; 
C correctly captures vicarious liability for Mark but wrongly denies vicarious liability for Lincoln; 
D correctly captures vicarious liability for Mark but wrongly treats minimal damage as a bar to Lincoln’s liability.

Unresolved nuance carried forward: whether “as he normally would” fully satisfies the standard of care/foreseeability for blower use immediately adjacent to a parked vehicle, and whether a stronger frolic argument could be made for Mark given the playing with dog, though consensus holds employment connection remains intact. No disagreement detected across rounds 0 and 1; unanimous E persists.

